5. SEGREGATE CUSTOMER, ADMINISTRATOR, AND AMBASSADOR AUTHENTICATION

Implement a secure role-aware authentication architecture.

Customers, administrators, and ambassadors must have distinct authorization boundaries.

They may share the underlying authentication provider if appropriate, but they must not share unrestricted access to protected business data.

Customer access

Customers can access their own:

- Profile.
- Orders.
- Addresses, if supported.
- Saved items, if supported.
- Customization requests, if supported.
- Account settings.

Customers must never access another customer's private information.

Administrator access

Administrator access must be granted through an authorized administrative process.

Public signup must never grant administrator permissions.

Do not create a publicly accessible form that allows users to select "Administrator" as a registration role.

Ambassador access

Ambassador access must be granted through a verified application and approval workflow, an authorized invitation, or another documented administrative process.

Public signup must not automatically approve an ambassador application.

Protected access behavior

When an ordinary customer opens an administrator URL:

- Do not expose administrator data.
- Deny the request on the server.
- Redirect to an appropriate access-denied or login page when appropriate.

When an ambassador opens an administrator URL:

- Deny access unless the account has separately been granted the relevant administrator permissions.

When an administrator opens the customer-facing website:

- Preserve ordinary browsing functionality.
- Provide an appropriate administrator entry point based on their authorized role.
- Do not expose privileged controls to ordinary customers.

Do not rely on a hidden navigation link to protect a page.

Every protected API operation must verify authorization independently.

